[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-161158"] :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,2z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2x+3y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\sqrt{14}.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=2x+3y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-2,\\,-3,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨2,\\,3,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-2,\\,-3,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-2,\\,-3,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}⟨-2,\\,-3,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2x+3y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,2z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}⟨-2,\\,-3,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}⟨x,\\,y,\\,4x+6y⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,-3,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}(-2x-3y+4x+6y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}(2x+3y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}(2x+3y)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}(2x+3y)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}(2x+3y)\\cdot \\sqrt{14}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{14}}}(2x+3y)\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{14}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x+3y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x+3y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}+3xy]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1+3y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[y+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-161187/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "d6b3e57d-7eec-562b-a2d8-88261bbb1678" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161187/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "073798a1-6b92-5611-b70a-76e7c0b692dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161187/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "ed19b014-3dfe-5988-a2b2-db5392654afc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161187/selection/e326a281f601aec64d5dcce4921033c5f93cbf7f8b0a7a5809a9962dfadde666" :answer/id #uuid "c7905471-7089-5b0e-a58c-5381d2c5bf90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161187/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer/id #uuid "5eb85ba7-210f-564f-9e9f-8552c2d35560" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161187/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "803b991c-eae9-5c86-a8af-24ba02361d31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161187/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer-field/choices ["q-161187/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-161187/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-161187/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" "q-161187/selection/e326a281f601aec64d5dcce4921033c5f93cbf7f8b0a7a5809a9962dfadde666" "q-161187/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161187" :question/id #uuid "319662ec-c01c-5bc5-9295-3611e5d3afa6" :question/math-academy-id "q-161187" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨4y,\\,5x,\\,2z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=3$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $1\\le y\\le 3.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=3$ for  $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $1\\le y\\le 3,$ the upward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=3$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨4y,\\,5x,\\,2z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4y,\\,5x,\\,6⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}6\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =6{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot (3-1)\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-161187/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3da3cc4f-0a1d-5639-b580-95eabc198d2a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161187"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161195/selection/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" :answer/id #uuid "97160bbe-9225-587d-84d6-6af779e74f1b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161195/selection/9c6bfb4db8d042ca3b93589c844ac1c789909e0099f428d8083546a00b4d8cf7" :answer/id #uuid "e88a5580-5343-5117-99fb-d85fd9b7f5de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161195/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer/id #uuid "d1b1b08e-1d05-523c-b86f-a5b05f295ffc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161195/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "79fd1428-07b4-5082-ac2a-6536a2b8727d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161195/selection/e3b46c3e63ee8ef5a071cd8ef5f7250114c0c6983477af061861d82a06039d46" :answer/id #uuid "8b990648-99d5-5958-a6e8-523415e858e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161195/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a009f081-1a97-54b6-880f-9c71ee87926c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161195/selection/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" :answer-field/choices ["q-161195/selection/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" "q-161195/selection/9c6bfb4db8d042ca3b93589c844ac1c789909e0099f428d8083546a00b4d8cf7" "q-161195/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" "q-161195/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-161195/selection/e3b46c3e63ee8ef5a071cd8ef5f7250114c0c6983477af061861d82a06039d46"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161195" :question/id #uuid "83d2d64f-ef6c-54fd-8a2d-e9caec911649" :question/math-academy-id "q-161195" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨-\\frac{{y}^{2}+1}{2},\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2},\\,z-1⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2x-2y+1$ for $-1\\le x\\le 0$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=3.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=2x-2y+1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨2,\\,-2,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2x-2y+1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨-\\frac{{y}^{2}+1}{2},\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2},\\,z-1⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-\\frac{{y}^{2}+1}{2},\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2},\\,2x-2y⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({y}^{2}+1+{x}^{2}+1+2x-2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[({y}^{2}-2y+1)+({x}^{2}+2x+1)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{3}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{3}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{3}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{3}}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[(x+1)^{2}\\cdot y+\\frac{(y-1)^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{3}+\\frac{x}{3}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-161195/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9a62365-4278-557f-ba76-caa31abe7869"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161195"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161196/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "6f95f533-4021-5306-87e7-0c30edb155c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161196/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "2993db3b-1f8a-5445-a7c3-97ccdfb158ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161196/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "1912c796-b407-53e2-88a2-6bc2cd13add7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161196/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "e61cfc99-111f-5f0f-a4f3-b0cf0ef49716" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161196/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "56439c31-61a7-5245-9ce7-83f18c3171d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161196/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "35be2174-3427-5dd8-9d58-5e8811fb2c32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161196/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer-field/choices ["q-161196/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" "q-161196/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-161196/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-161196/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-161196/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161196" :question/id #uuid "b49439b2-a074-558f-9e48-de199d0a401a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161196" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,z,\\,4xy⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=1$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is a continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=1$ for  $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1,$ the downward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,z,\\,4xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,4xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{[{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-161196/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3da3cc4f-0a1d-5639-b580-95eabc198d2a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161196"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-162551/selection/4644593a6ae896793c595e8a37cd468ba91be6a8038e715c4c88cddad1968793" :answer/id #uuid "5528c82a-1aba-5b62-95b2-c3453eaff52f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162551/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer/id #uuid "92b137c1-6170-55d8-9953-d0a8c1b1b7a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162551/selection/f509030681214332b5989836fe0af4b2eb5858d193e689fd8441dff5cd45ff45" :answer/id #uuid "bd33d004-f78c-53f0-89f6-6d3db366b20f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162551/selection/e326a281f601aec64d5dcce4921033c5f93cbf7f8b0a7a5809a9962dfadde666" :answer/id #uuid "cc61d927-b49a-50ee-a68e-d42ce868fe75" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162551/selection/6d8a7c7e73b503e82ee3fdeaeeda8131c0106d9ecb6a16ba218483b62acd0090" :answer/id #uuid "ecb1197a-f48b-5bba-a55b-63af6b6b0c29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162551/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3fb79bbb-124a-5f13-b63f-e0a26d3e2b92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162551/selection/e326a281f601aec64d5dcce4921033c5f93cbf7f8b0a7a5809a9962dfadde666" :answer-field/choices ["q-162551/selection/4644593a6ae896793c595e8a37cd468ba91be6a8038e715c4c88cddad1968793" "q-162551/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" "q-162551/selection/f509030681214332b5989836fe0af4b2eb5858d193e689fd8441dff5cd45ff45" "q-162551/selection/e326a281f601aec64d5dcce4921033c5f93cbf7f8b0a7a5809a9962dfadde666" "q-162551/selection/6d8a7c7e73b503e82ee3fdeaeeda8131c0106d9ecb6a16ba218483b62acd0090"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162551" :question/id #uuid "1c160107-8805-58b0-9a37-d31f8eeb4e88" :question/math-academy-id "q-162551" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨3x,\\,2z,\\,y-2⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2x-y+1$ for $0\\le x\\le 4$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\sqrt{6}.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=2x-y+1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-2,\\,1,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince we require a downward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is negative. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2x-y+1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨3x,\\,2z,\\,y-2⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨3x,\\,4x-2y+2,\\,y-2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(6x-(4x-2y+2)-(y-2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+y)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+y)\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}}(2x+y)\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}+xy]}_{x=0}^{x=4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4y+16)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[2{y}^{2}+16y]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-162551/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9a62365-4278-557f-ba76-caa31abe7869"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162551"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
